 do because you  have to be able to observe Jupiter and  see the moons and measure their  positions very accurately so it wasn't  terribly useful for that but nonetheless  a whole load of measurements were made  of the positions of the moons of Jupiter  and people found that it did indeed work  as a very accurate clock except that  sometimes it seemed to run a bit slow  and sometimes it seemed to run a bit far  sometimes it seemed to be a bit behind  where you're expecting it to be  sometimes you seem to be it seemed to be  a bit ahead and in fact it was Cassini  the guy who found the gaps in the rings  of Saturn and lots of other astronomical  things who first figured out the  explanation for this which is that he  figured out the reason for this was  because of the light that was showing us  what was going on so the light was  traveling to us from Jupiter was taking  a finite amount of time to get to us and  so when Jupiter is a long way away from  us in the solar system on its orbit then  the light takes that bit  longer and so we see the clock a bit  further in the past when you put us  quite close to us we see it pretty much  as it is now and therefore there isn't  that time delay and so it was realized  you could actually use this to measure  the speed of light by looking at what  these time delays were at different  times you could see how long it was  actually taking the light to get to us  if you knew how far away Jupiter was you  could use all that to figure out the  speed of light since the 1600s again in  1660 ish someone looked at Jupiter with  the most powerful telescope at the time  and found a dot on it which is what  we've come to call the Great Red Spot  which is a massive storm on Jupiter so  big that you could actually fit two  Earth's inside of this storm and it's  lasted for over 300 years now the planet  has a lot of gas it's a gas giant so  it's mostly hydrogen and there's some  helium and a couple other things mixed  in there but it's all gas is far down  hundreds of miles as we've been able to  probe into the surface and this gas is  moving around quite quickly Jupiter  rotates on its axis very quickly in 10  hours which for such a huge planet is  really fast so what that causes is that  all the gas on the surface sort of  separates into layers so if you look at  Jupiter at an image you see almost  stripes horizontally along the planet  and they are dark and they're bright so  Jupiter has some well-known moons  Ganymede is one moon and it's the  biggest moon in the entire solar system  it's actually bigger than mercury so  that one's exciting it's got craters on  it so EO is the closest moon and it's a  very big moon and since it's so close to  Jupiter it interacts with it strongly  gravitationally and with its magnetic  field and so the result of that is a lot  of volcanic activities so volcanoes  erupted all the time there's a lot of  sulfur not much chance for life or  anything there with as much activity as  it has going on now but Europa on the  other hand is actually a place in our  solar system where we could potentially  have life now the surface is very smooth  it's a big thick layer of I  then we see cracks along the surface in  the ice but the ice is so thick it's  maybe 50 to 100 miles thick and  potentially underneath there there could  be liquid oceans with things growing I  mean we don't really know it's not  likely but we do have life-forms at in  the depths of the ocean near volcanoes  on earth so there could potentially be  something there in 1994 we found the  first comet orbiting around something  other than the Sun so this comment was  called shoemaker-levy 9 and it was found  to be orbiting around Jupiter and it has  very elliptical very long orbit and so  and one of the passes pretty close to  after we detected it it went very close  to Jupiter and so it was predicted that  it was going to actually run into  Jupiter and so this was not only the  first comment that we had ever found  orbiting and something other than the  Sun but it was potentially going to  crash in to Jupiter so it would be the  first time we'd ever see something like  that the first point of contact when it  crashed into the southern part of  Jupiter was on the other side of us so  we couldn't see it but we had spacecraft  that actually monitored that impact and  then it rotated so quickly  that within an hour - we could actually  see the sights of the impact so we  weren't sure what was going to happen we  didn't know if there's a surface  somewhere down in the depths of Jupiter  that we could see or if these pieces  would just be completely engulfed by the  atmosphere so we couldn't see anything  but we did end up seeing some reactions  we saw some really dark spots forming  there were a successive period of 5 days  where these pieces hit Jupiter and you  could see these big dark clouds forming  and they just slowly faded over time so  that was a pretty exciting thing when I  was when I was young and I was just  thinking about my interest in astronomy  that was one of the things that I  remember happening in real time and it  was just fantastically exciting

